# Chapter 15 · From the MDGs to the SDGs: The Global Goals and Pakistan

> MCQs ask which goal targets gender equality (**SDG 5**), which item was **not** an MDG, and about a South Asian meeting on SDGs and women (**Kathmandu, 2017**). This chapter gives you the global goals and Pakistan's record.

## 15.1  The Millennium Development Goals (2000–2015)

The **Millennium Declaration** (September ==2000==) led to **eight Millennium Development Goals** for ==2015==:

::: table | The eight MDGs.
@widths 1.2,12.8
No. | Goal
1 | Eradicate extreme poverty and hunger
2 | Achieve universal primary education
3 | **Promote gender equality and empower women** (main target: eliminate gender disparity in primary and secondary education)
4 | Reduce child mortality
5 | **Improve maternal health**
6 | Combat HIV/AIDS, malaria and other diseases
7 | Ensure environmental sustainability
8 | Develop a global partnership for development
:::

**Criticism from a gender angle:** MDG 3 focused narrowly on **education parity**; it left out violence against women, unpaid care, property rights and reproductive rights (added only later as a target under MDG 5). A 2025 MCQ asked which option was **not** an MDG — "equal political participation of women" was not one of the eight goals (though women's share of parliamentary seats was an **indicator** under MDG 3).

**Pakistan and the MDGs:** Pakistan missed most MDG targets, including those on primary education, gender parity and maternal mortality.

## 15.2  The Sustainable Development Goals (2015–2030)

The **2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development**, adopted in September ==2015==, set **17 SDGs** and **169 targets**, with the promise to "**leave no one behind**". Unlike the MDGs, they apply to **all** countries, and gender equality is both a **stand-alone goal** and a theme across the others.

::: define SDG 5 | the gender goal
"**Achieve gender equality and empower all women and girls.**" (MCQ 2026: the SDG that specifically targets gender equality is SDG 5.)
:::

::: table | The targets of SDG 5.
@widths 1.2,12.8
Target | Content
5.1 | End all forms of **discrimination** against women and girls
5.2 | Eliminate all forms of **violence** against women and girls, including trafficking and sexual exploitation
5.3 | Eliminate harmful practices such as **child, early and forced marriage** and female genital mutilation
5.4 | Recognise and value **unpaid care and domestic work**
5.5 | Ensure women's full participation and **equal leadership** in political, economic and public life
5.6 | Universal access to **sexual and reproductive health** and reproductive rights
5.a | Equal rights to **economic resources**, land and property
5.b | Use **technology**, especially ICT, to empower women
5.c | Adopt and strengthen **policies and laws** for gender equality
:::

::: fig mapping | How SDG 5 targets connect to Pakistani laws and programmes.
@left SDG 5 target
@right Pakistani response
5.2 Violence => Harassment Act 2010; honour law 2016; Anti-Rape Act 2021
5.3 Child marriage => Sindh 2013; Islamabad 2025 (age 18)
5.4 Unpaid care => BISP cash to women; time-use data
5.5 Leadership => 60 NA reserved seats; Elections Act 2017
5.a Property => Women's Property Rights Act 2020
5.b Technology => Digital inclusion; mobile gap down to 25%
:::

## 15.3  Pakistan and the SDGs

- In **February 2016** the National Assembly unanimously adopted the SDGs as Pakistan's **national development agenda** — one of the first parliaments to do so. SDG support units were set up federally and in the provinces.
- A regional meeting of South Asian stakeholders on SDGs and women was held in **Kathmandu** in ==2017== (MCQ 2018).
- **Progress on SDG 5** is slow: Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== on the Global Gender Gap Index (2026); female labour-force participation is ==24.4%==; the maternal mortality ratio was ==186== per 100,000 live births (2019); child marriage remains common in many areas.
- **Positive steps:** new laws (2020–2025), BISP, girls' enrolment gains, women's rising use of mobile phones.

## 15.3a  Gender across the other SDGs

Gender equality is not only in SDG 5. Other goals have gender targets or depend on women:

- **SDG 1** (poverty) — equal rights to economic resources and social protection;
- **SDG 3** (health) — maternal mortality and reproductive health;
- **SDG 4** (education) — equal access to all levels of education;
- **SDG 6** (water and sanitation) — attention to the needs of women and girls, who collect most water;
- **SDG 8** (decent work) — equal pay for work of equal value;
- **SDG 13** (climate) — women's role in climate planning;
- **SDG 16** (peace and justice) — ending violence and inclusive decision-making.

This "**gender across the goals**" approach is gender mainstreaming applied to the global agenda.

## 15.4  Assessment of the goals approach

::: balance | Have global goals helped women?
- **Yes:** they created **targets, data and attention**; they pushed governments to report; MDG 3 helped close gender gaps in primary schooling in many countries; SDG 5 is far broader.
- **But:** goals are **voluntary**; many countries, including Pakistan, lack data; targets on violence, unpaid care and property are hard to measure; financing is short; and the COVID-19 pandemic (==2020==–21) set back progress. UN Women reports suggest that, at current rates, full gender equality under SDG 5 will not be reached by 2030.
-> Goals help most when **domestic politics** — women's movements, legislators, courts — use them to press governments.
:::

::: eye | Using this chapter
Use SDG 5 and its targets in any Topic 4 conclusion: they turn a critique into a **policy agenda**. Link each recommendation to a target (5.2 violence, 5.4 unpaid care, 5.a land). Examiners reward answers that end with concrete, internationally anchored policy.
:::
